1) 从告警标签取 laddr_port/raddr_ip 等过滤条件，执行 s:
1) Take the filter conditions (laddr_port, raddr_ip, ...) from the alert labels and run ss -tanp state close-wait '( sport = :<port> )' to find the process holding the connections;
2) Compare ls -l /proc/<pid>/fd | wc -l against cat /proc/<pid>/limits to see how much fd headroom remains;
3) CLOSE_WAIT is always an application bug: check whether an error path forgets to close the connection, or whether an HTTP client fails to drain and close the response body;
4) Restarting the process releases them as an emergency measure, but the code must be fixed or it will recur.

1) 执行 ss -s 与 netstat -s | grep -i 'SYNs:
1) Run ss -s and netstat -s | grep -i 'SYNs to LISTEN' to see whether SYN drop counters are increasing;
2) If legitimate traffic is filling the queue, raise net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog and the application's listen backlog, and raise somaxconn to match;
3) If you suspect a SYN flood, enable net.ipv4.tcp_syncookies=1 and aggregate source IPs from ss -tan state syn-recv to rate-limit them at the firewall;
4) A slow accept loop in the backend also shows up as SYN_RECV build-up, so check whether the application is blocked inside its accept loop.

1) 执行 ss -tan state time-wait | wc -l 确认:
1) Run ss -tan state time-wait | wc -l to size the problem and cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_local_port_range to see the available port range;
2) The root cause is normally too many short-lived connections; enabling connection pooling and HTTP Keep-Alive in the application is the only real fix;
3) On the kernel side you may enable net.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse=1 (only helps the connection initiator); do not enable the deprecated tcp_tw_recycle, which drops packets behind NAT;
4) If needed, widen ip_local_port_range and raise net.ipv4.tcp_max_tw_buckets to relieve pressure.
